{"batchcomplete":"","query":{"normalized":[{"from":"White_Beast_Statue","to":"White Beast Statue"},{"from":"Odd_Mushroom","to":"Odd Mushroom"},{"from":"Teardrop_Locket","to":"Teardrop Locket"},{"from":"Philosopher's_Stone","to":"Philosopher's Stone"},{"from":"Runic_Capacitor","to":"Runic Capacitor"},{"from":"Pandora's_Box","to":"Pandora's Box"},{"from":"Orange_Pellets","to":"Orange Pellets"},{"from":"Neow's_Lament","to":"Neow's Lament"},{"from":"N'loth's_Gift","to":"N'loth's Gift"},{"from":"Black_Blood","to":"Black Blood"},{"from":"Ornamental_Fan","to":"Ornamental Fan"},{"from":"Oddly_Smooth_Stone","to":"Oddly Smooth Stone"},{"from":"Pen_Nib","to":"Pen Nib"},{"from":"N'loth's_Hungry_Face","to":"N'loth's Hungry Face"},{"from":"Cloak_Clasp","to":"Cloak Clasp"},{"from":"Preserved_Insect","to":"Preserved Insect"}],"pages":{"2397":{"pageid":2397,"ns":0,"title":"Anchor","revisions":[{"revid":56316,"parentid":50800,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation||1}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Anchor\n| Image       = Anchor.png\n| Rarity      = Common\n| Description = Start each combat with 10 {{KW|Block}}.\n| Flavor      = Holding this miniature trinket, you feel heavier and more stable.\n| GivesToPlayer = Block\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}}\n{{R|Anchor}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Anchor}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Anchor}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Anchor}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Anchor}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Anchor}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Anchor}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Anchor}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Anchor}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Anchor}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Anchor}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* By guaranteeing some {{KW|Block}} during the first turn, {{R|Anchor}} synergises well with the {{KW|Innate}} {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} cards (such as {{C|Dramatic Entrance}}, {{C|Mind Blast}} and {{C|Backstab}}), as well as other '''Attack''' cards made Innate by {{R|Bottled Flame}}.\n* The {{KW|Block}} can help delay the activation of {{BD|Buffer}} gained through {{R|Fossilized Helix}} or {{C|Buffer}}.\n* Against enemies that do 10 or less damage on the first turn, {{R|Anchor}} completely neutralises damage, freeing up the {{KW|Energy}} that would have been spent on defence.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Anchor}} makes it more difficult to trigger {{R|Centennial Puzzle}}, should you want to.\n* The {{KW|Block}} is wasted on enemies that do no damage on the first turn, unless the '''block''' is carried over to the next turn using effects like {{C|Barricade}}, {{C|Blur}}, and {{R|Calipers}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Anchor}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic in a set of relics which all grant {{KW|Block}} on different turns of combat; the others being the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} {{R|Horn Cleat}} on turn 2 and the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} {{R|Captain's Wheel}} on turn 3.\n* An anchor is a nautical device used to secure a ship (or other sea-faring vessel) in place by preventing it from drifting due to strong winds or currents.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchor</ref>\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1446":{"pageid":1446,"ns":0,"title":"Black Blood","revisions":[{"revid":55391,"parentid":50938,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation|1}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Black Blood\n|Image       = BlackBlood.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Character   = Ironclad\n|Description = Replaces {{R|Burning Blood}}. At the end of combat, heal 12 HP.\n|Flavor      = The rage grows darker.\n}}\n{{R|Black Blood}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Ironclad|Ironclad-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. It's the upgraded form of {{R|Burning Blood}}, and replaces it entirely when acquired from a Boss Chest.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be obtained as a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}} choice, if the player has {{R|Burning Blood}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Ironclad]] while they possess {{R|Burning Blood}}.\n* Can never be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Black Blood}}'s healing happens at the end of combat ''before'' the combat rewards screen opens, so it benefits from {{R|Magic Flower}}'s healing buff (healing for 18 HP instead of the normal 12 HP).\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As with all healing effects, {{R|Black Blood}} restores no HP if the player possesses {{R|Mark of the Bloom}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Black Blood relic buffed. Heal increased from 10 -> 12.\n* '''Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!'''\n** Black Blood, Burning Blood, and Meat on the Bone no longer heal you if you are dead.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** Replacing relics now retains the array order, fixing Bloody Idol + Black Blood issue.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3053":{"pageid":3053,"ns":0,"title":"Cloak Clasp","revisions":[{"revid":56939,"parentid":56938,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Cloak Clasp\n|Image       = CloakClasp.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Character   = Watcher\n|Description = At the end of your turn, gain 1 {{KW|Block}} for each card in your hand.\n|Flavor      = A simple but sturdy design.\n|GivesToPlayer = Block\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}}\n{{R|Cloak Clasp}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Watcher|Watcher-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Cloak Clasp}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Cloak Clasp}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Cloak Clasp}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Cloak Clasp}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Cloak Clasp}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Cloak Clasp}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Cloak Clasp}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Cloak Clasp}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Cloak Clasp}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Cloak Clasp}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Cloak Clasp}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Cloak Clasp}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained by [[Watcher]].\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Cloak Clasp}} will trigger ''after'' {{R|Orichalcum}} has checked whether the player has 0 {{KW|Block}}, allowing both relics to be used together effectively.\n* {{R|Cloak Clasp}} will trigger ''before'' cards in hand trigger effects that cause them to leave the hand before the normal discard timing (such as cards with {{KW|Ethereal}}, or cards which damage the player at the end of the turn such as {{C|Burn}} or {{C|Regret}}).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* [[Watcher]] has many cards that {{KW|Retain}} (such as {{C|Crescendo}}, {{C|Flying Sleeves}}, and {{C|Sands of Time}}) which can be kept in hand to increase the {{KW|Block}} gain from {{R|Cloak Clasp}} over multiple turns.\n* An {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} {{C|Meditate}}+ can be used to pull 2 cards from the discard pile into the hand, providing 1 extra {{KW|Block}} from {{R|Cloak Clasp}} compared to leaving it unplayed in the hand.\n* {{R|Cloak Clasp}} will grant {{KW|Block}} even if the player has the {{BD|No Block}} debuff from {{C|Panic Button}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* The description of {{R|Cloak Clasp}} can be somewhat misleading. All the {{KW|Block}} from ''\"gain 1 {{KW|Block}} for each card in your hand\"'' is gained together as one instance, so a card such as {{C|Juggernaut}} will only trigger once even if the player has many cards in their hand.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Watcher only, Rare Relic: Cloak Clasp\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2496":{"pageid":2496,"ns":0,"title":"Matryoshka","revisions":[{"revid":55457,"parentid":50848,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Matryoshka\n|Image       = Matryoshka.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = The next 2 non-boss chests you open contain 2 Relics.\n|Flavor      = A stackable set of painted dolls. The paint depicts an unknown bird with white eyes and blue feathers.\n}}\n{{R|Matryoshka}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. Each extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} and 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}. Only the bottom-most relic in a [[Chest]] may be linked to the [[Mechanics#Keys|Sapphire Key]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Matryoshka}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Matryoshka}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Matryoshka}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Matryoshka}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Matryoshka}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Matryoshka}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Matryoshka}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Matryoshka}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Matryoshka}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 41 (2 floors below the mid-Act [[Mechanics#Chests|Treasure room]] in [[Act 3]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Tiny Chest}} can allow the extra relics provided by {{R|Matryoshka}} to be obtained sooner than normal thanks to its extra [[Map Locations#Treasure Room|Treasure Room]] generation.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Having both {{R|Matryoshka}} and {{R|N'loth's Hungry Face}} at the same time will yield a [[Chest|chest]] with 1 [[Relic]] in it. These two relics effectively cancel each other's effects. If the player has not yet collected the [[Mechanics#Keys|Sapphire Key]], then the relic will be linked to it as normal.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* It can be awkward to benefit from the extra relic from {{R|Matryoshka}} while also having {{R|Cursed Key}} since it will add a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} to the player's deck (unless the player also has {{R|Omamori}} to prevent gaining the curse).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The flavor text mentioning ''an unknown bird with white eyes and blue feathers'' references the [[Bosses#Awakened One|Awakened One]].\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Relics which gray out are now grayed out using a shader, rather than a grayscale image.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Matryoshka relic no longer spawns beyond floor 40 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act'''\n** Fixed issue where Nloth's Face relic consumed both relics from Matryoshka.\n* '''Weekly Patch 21: Channel Lightning'''\n** Seeds are now utilized for Matryoshka and Mummified Hand relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 7: Setting Up All the Things'''\n** Fixing typo in Matryoshka's flavor text.\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** Matryoshka's counters are now saved correctly- no longer resets.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3141":{"pageid":3141,"ns":0,"title":"N'loth's Gift","revisions":[{"revid":51026,"parentid":50990,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = N'loth's Gift\n|Image       = NlothsGift.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = N'loth\n|Description = Triples the chance of finding {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} cards from combat rewards.\n|Flavor      = The strange gift from N'loth. Whenever you try and unwrap it, another wrapped box of the same size lies within.\n}}\n{{R|N'loth's Gift}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]]. It multiplies the base chance for a card to be rare by 3, ''before'' subtracting the 5% offset penalty. For more information, see [[#Effects on Rare Card Chance|Effects on Rare Card Chance]].\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|N'loth}} by selecting the {{Frame|Offer}} option and trading one of two randomly selected [[Relics]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n* {{R|N'loth's Gift}} will \"flash\" whenever a {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} card is offered that would '''not''' have been offered without the increased rare card chance.\n* {{E|N'loth}} chooses 2 [[Relics]] indiscriminately that the player possesses; even having a chance to ask for their {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Starter|Starter relic}} or a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss relic}}.\n\n=== Effects on Rare Card Chance ===\n''For more information on {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} card chance, see [[Cards#Card_Distribution|Card Distribution]].''\n\nDue to how {{R|N'loth's Gift}} multiplies the ''base'' chance for a card to be {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} by 3, and not the ''final'' {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} chance after the 5% offset penalty is applied, its effect can be better or worse than simply multiplying the final chance of seeing a {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} card by 3 depending on the scenario.\n\nFor example, the base chance of finding a {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} card from a normal combat is 3%. After the 5% offset penalty, the base chance is -2% (the player must see 3 {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Common|common}} cards after a {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} card before it becomes possible to see another {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} card). With {{R|N'loth's Gift}} tripling the base chance from 3% to 9%, and then the 5% offset penalty bringing the chance to 4%, the player can find multiple {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} cards back-to-back from normal card rewards (which is normally impossible).\n\nThis also means the ''\"Triples the chance of finding {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} cards\"'' ends up behaving as more than the described 3x multiplier if the player has not seen many {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Common|common}} cards. The actual formula with {{R|N'loth's Gift}} is <code>(Base Chance * 3) - 5% + (1% * number of Commons seen since last Rare)</code>. Consider the following table:\n\n{| class=\"wikitable\"\n|+ Chance of Next Card Offered Being Rare\n|-\n! Commons Seen Since Last Rare !! Normal Combat (base 3%)<br>(no relic) !! Normal Combat (base 3%)<br>({{R|N'loth's Gift}}) !! Normal Combat<br>Actual Multiplier !! Elite Combat (base 10%)<br>(no relic) !! Elite Combat (base 10%)<br>({{R|N'loth's Gift}}) !! Elite Combat<br>Actual Multiplier !! Shop (base 9%)<br>(no relic) !! Shop (base 9%)<br>({{R|N'loth's Gift}}) !! Shop<br>Actual Multiplier\n|-\n| 0 (base offset chance) || -2% (0%) || 4% || N/A (\u221ex) || 5% || 25% || 5x || 4% || 22% || 5.5x\n|-\n| 1 || -1% (0%) || 5% || N/A (\u221ex) || 6% || 26% || ~4.33x || 5% || 23% || 4.6x\n|-\n| 2 || 0% || 6% || N/A (\u221ex) || 7% || 27% || 3.86x || 6% || 24% || 4x\n|-\n| 3 (1 card reward all Commons) || 1% || 7% || 7x || 8% || 28% || 3.5x || 7% || 25% || ~3.57x\n|-\n| 4 || 2% || 8% || 4x || 7% || 27% || ~3.22x || 8% || 26% || 3.25x\n|-\n| 5 || 3% || 9% || {{TS|gold||3x}} || 10% || 30% || {{TS|gold||3x}} || 9% || 27% || {{TS|gold||3x}}\n|-\n| 6 (2 card rewards all Commons) || 4% || 10% || 2.5x || 11% || 31% || ~2.82x || 10% || 28% || 2.8x\n|-\n| 7 || 5% || 11% || 2.2x || 12% || 32% || ~2.67x || 11% || 29% || ~2.64x\n|-\n| 8 || 6% || 12% || 2x || 13% || 33% || ~2.54x || 12% || 30% || 2.5x\n|-\n| 9 (3 card rewards all Commons) || 7% || 13% || ~1.86x || 14% || 34% || ~2.43x || 13% || 31% || ~2.38x\n|}\n\n{{R|N'loth's Gift}} is better than the described \"tripled chance\" from its description if the player has seen fewer than 5 {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Common|common}} cards since their last {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} card. It is equal to or worse than the described \"tripled chance\" if the player has seen at least 5 {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Common|common}} cards since their last {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|rare}} card.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* [[Events]] that allow the player to receive card rewards (such as {{E|Sensory Stone}}) or choose cards to add to their deck (such as {{E|The Library}}) will benefit from the increased rare chance.\n* There are some {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} cards that are very powerful in most decks regardless of their overall strategy (such as {{C|Offering}}, {{C|Adrenaline}}, {{C|Seek}}, and {{C|Scrawl}}). {{R|N'loth's Gift}} can allow the player to find these generally powerful cards more frequently.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* There are some {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Rare|Rare}} cards that are situational or difficult to use without support (such as {{C|Barricade}}, {{C|Grand Finale}}, {{C|Meteor Strike}}, and {{C|Master Reality}}). The player may not want to increase their chances of finding these cards if their deck already has a winning strategy that most rare cards will not help refine.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** N'loth's Gift was flashing at incorrect times.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** N'loth's Gift actually works now.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3128":{"pageid":3128,"ns":0,"title":"N'loth's Hungry Face","revisions":[{"revid":51027,"parentid":50991,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = N'loth's Hungry Face\n|Image       = NlothsMask.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = N'loth\n|Description = The next non-Boss chest you open is empty.\n|Flavor      = You feel hungry.\n}}\n{{R|N'loth's Hungry Face}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]]. The next non-Boss [[Chest]] that the player opens will contain no [[Gold]] and no [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Face Trader}} by selecting the {{Frame|Trade}} option and randomly receiving {{R|N'loth's Hungry Face}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|N'loth's Hungry Face}} can be traded for {{R|N'loth's Gift}} if it's randomly chosen in the {{E|N'loth}} [[Event]]. This will allow the next non-Boss [[Chest]] to have its normal contents.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* Having both {{R|Matryoshka}} and {{R|N'loth's Hungry Face}} at the same time will yield a chest with 1 relic in it. If the player has not yet collected the [[Mechanics#Keys|Sapphire Key]], then the relic will be linked to it as normal. The two relics effectively cancel each other's effects.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Internally called <code>NlothsMask</code>.\n* {{R|N'loth's Hungry Face}} is the face of {{E|N'loth}} that was taken by the {{E|Face Trader}}. There is no special event dialogue for trading N'loth their own face.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Relics which gray out are now grayed out using a shader, rather than a grayscale image.\n* '''Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act'''\n** Fixed issue where Nloth's Face relic consumed both relics from Matryoshka.\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n** Fixed naming on N'Loth's Hungry Face to be consistent with other usages of N'Loth's name.\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Face Trader event will no longer give out duplicate relics on Endless mode.\n* '''Weekly Patch 32: Face Trader'''\n** Adding event relic N'loth's Hungry Face.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3132":{"pageid":3132,"ns":0,"title":"Neow's Lament","revisions":[{"revid":55469,"parentid":51028,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Neow's Lament\n|Image       = NeowsBlessing.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Neow\n|Description = Enemies in your first 3 combats will have 1 HP.\n|Flavor      = The blessing of lamentation bestowed by Neow.\n}}\n{{R|Neow's Lament}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]]. It sets the HP to 1 for ALL enemies in the next 3 combats. This applies to combats of any type; [[Slay the Spire Wiki/Enemies|normal enemies]], [[Elites|elites]], and [[Bosses|bosses]] (although it is quite difficult to reach a [[Boss]] with at least one charge remaining).\n\n== Sources ==\nCan be obtained from Neow's [[Neow#Second_Blessing|Second Blessing]] at the start of a run. It is also guaranteed as one of the two choices if the previous run for the chosen [[Slay the Spire Wiki/character|Character]] did not reach the boss of [[Act 1]].\n\n== Trivia ==\n* ''Lament'' means to mourn or experience grief over a loss. {{R|Neow's Lament}} can be interpreted as [[Neow]] taking pity on the player character for dying quite early into their previous run.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Relics which gray out are now grayed out using a shader, rather than a grayscale image.\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Neow's Lament relic now grays out when it is used up.\n* '''Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act'''\n** HP loss amount is now blue for Neow's Lament relic description.\n* '''Weekly Patch 46: Adrenaline'''\n** We created Neow with the intent to foster creative deckbuilding. However, many have felt that the game was considerably harder without a blessing from Neow and the threshold to receive her blessing was too high (especially in higher Ascensions). To compensate, we've added a fixed set of choices for those who don't reach the Act 1 boss (Neow's Lament or HP bonus).\n* '''Weekly Patch 42: Reboot'''\n** Fixed issue where Lagavulin would come out of its shell with Neow's Lament or Preserved Insect.\n* '''Weekly Patch 7: Setting Up All the Things'''\n** Adding a used up image for Neow's Lament.\n* '''Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!'''\n** Neow now offers a new option: Neow's Lament.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3139":{"pageid":3139,"ns":0,"title":"Odd Mushroom","revisions":[{"revid":55875,"parentid":51016,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Odd Mushroom\n|Image       = OddMushroom.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Hypnotizing Colored Mushrooms\n|Description = When {{BD|Vulnerable}}, take 25% more attack damage rather than 50%.\n|Flavor      = \"After consuming trichella parastius I felt larger and less... susceptible.\" - Ranwid \n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Vulnerable}}\n{{R|Odd Mushroom}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Hypnotizing Colored Mushrooms}} by selecting the {{Frame|Stomp}} option and defeating the 3 {{M|Fungi Beast|Fungi Beasts}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\nWhen calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{BD|Vulnerable}} is a multiplicative bonus.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* The negative effect of playing {{C|Berserk}} is lessened since it is not as punishing for the player to be {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n* There are many dangerous enemies that apply {{BD|Vulnerable}} to the player, such as {{M|Gremlin Nob}}, {{M|The Collector}}, {{M|Writhing Mass}}, and the {{M|Corrupt Heart}}. {{R|Odd Mushroom}} can make the {{BD|Vulnerable}} turns of these fights more manageable.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Odd Mushroom}} may be a reference to the [https://bindingofisaacrebirth.wiki.gg/wiki/Odd_Mushroom_(Disambiguation) item of the same name] from [https://bindingofisaacrebirth.wiki.gg/ The Binding of Isaac: Rebirth].\n** Its flavor text may be a reference to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amanita_muscaria fly agaric], the mushroom that the iconic [https://www.mariowiki.com/Mushroom Mushroom from the Mario franchise] is based off of, as it is known for its [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amanita_muscaria#Psychoactive psychoactive effects] that can make the user feel like they are growing taller.\n* {{R|Odd Mushroom}} is one of the relics in the \"edible relic set\" that all reduce [[Debuffs|debuffs]]; the others being {{R|Ginger}} for blocking {{BD|Weak}} and {{R|Turnip}} for blocking {{BD|Frail}}.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2526":{"pageid":2526,"ns":0,"title":"Oddly Smooth Stone","revisions":[{"revid":55876,"parentid":50816,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Oddly Smooth Stone\n|Image       = OddlySmoothStone.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = At the start of each combat, gain 1 {{BD|Dexterity}}.\n|Flavor      = You have never seen something so smooth and pristine. This must be the work of the Ancients.\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Dexterity}}{{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Oddly Smooth Stone}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Both instances of {{KW|Block}} gain from {{C|Dodge and Roll}} will benefit from the ''current'' {{BD|Dexterity}}.\n** I.e. The {{KW|Block}} gained on the next turn after {{C|Dodge and Roll}} is played will be completely unaffected by changes to the amount of {{BD|Dexterity}} the player has when their next turn starts.\n* Excess {{KW|Block}} can be carried to the next turn using effects from {{C|Barricade}}, {{C|Blur}}, or {{R|Calipers}}.\n* The improved {{KW|Block}} gain from playing cards can help reduce chip damage that becomes more dangerous with {{R|Coffee Dripper}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{BD|Dexterity}} does not affect the {{KW|Block}} gain for effects like {{C|Feel No Pain}} and {{C|After Image}} as the '''block''' does not come from playing a card that directly grants '''block''' on play.\n* {{BD|Dexterity}} is only applied to the final total of {{C|Spirit Shield}} and '''not''' the amount of {{KW|Block}} gained per card in hand.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 23: Testing Continues...'''\n** Wording improvements for Vajra and Oddly Smooth Stone relic.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2487":{"pageid":2487,"ns":0,"title":"Orange Pellets","revisions":[{"revid":55878,"parentid":50966,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Orange Pellets\n|Image       = OrangePellets.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Description = Whenever you play a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}}, {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}}, and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} in the same turn, remove all of your [[Debuffs|debuffs]].\n|Flavor      = Made from various fungi found throughout the Spire, they will stave off any affliction.\n}}\n{{R|Orange Pellets}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]]. For a full list of the effects that {{R|Orange Pellets}} can remove from the player, see [[Debuffs]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Interactions ==\n* The player is able to trigger {{R|Orange Pellets}} multiple times in the same turn, provided they can draw and play enough {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}}, {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}, and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skills}} in the same turn.\n* Playing multiple of the same card type does not \"stack\" for future {{R|Orange Pellets}} plays. The player must fully trigger {{R|Orange Pellets}} using all 3 card types before they can \"make progress\" towards the next trigger.\n** For example, playing 2 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}, then 1 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}}, then 1 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} will trigger {{R|Orange Pellets}} after the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} as expected. However, the \"extra\" {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} that was played earlier in the turn '''does not''' count towards the next trigger of {{R|Orange Pellets}}. The player will need to find and play all 3 card types again if they wish to trigger {{R|Orange Pellets}} a second time on this same turn.\n** If the player is confused, they can imagine 3 toggle switches (such as a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light_switch#Toggle light switch], a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Checkbox check box], a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boolean_data_type boolean variable], or whatever else helps them visualize) that each keep track of 1 out of the 3 card types. 1 switch for keeping track of whether an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} has been played yet, 1 for keeping track of whether a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} has been played yet, and 1 for keeping track of whether a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} has been played yet. Whenever all 3 toggle switches are \"on\", {{R|Orange Pellets}} will trigger, and then all 3 check boxes will be \"turned off\". It does not matter how ''many'' times a card type has been played before each {{R|Orange Pellets}} trigger, only that ''at least one card of each type'' has been played.\n* {{R|Orange Pellets}} will only remove any ''overall'' negative stat [[Debuffs]] (such as negative {{BD|Strength}}, negative {{BD|Dexterity}}, and negative {{BD|Focus}}). If other effects lower a stat, but the overall amount of that stat is still positive, then {{R|Orange Pellets}} will not \"restore\" the lost stats.\n** For example, if the player has 3 {{BD|Dexterity}} and then {{BD|Wraith Form}} reduces it to only 2 {{BD|Dexterity}}, triggering {{R|Orange Pellets}} will remove the {{BD|Wraith Form}} debuff but the player's {{BD|Dexterity}} will remain at 2.\n** Whereas if they had 0 {{BD|Dexterity}} and {{BD|Wraith Form}} reduces it to ''negative'' 1 {{BD|Dexterity}}, then triggering {{R|Orange Pellets}} will remove both the {{BD|Wraith Form}} and the negative {{BD|Dexterity}}.\n\n=== Order ===\n* The {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}}, {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}}, and {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} can be played in any order. The player does '''not''' need to follow the exact order in {{R|Orange Pellets}}' description.\n* {{R|Orange Pellets}} will trigger and remove [[Debuffs]] ''after'' the final card needed to trigger it has fully resolved.\n** For example, if any {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} and any {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Skill|Skill}} is played, and then a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} that applies a [[Debuff]] to the player (such as {{C|Berserk}}, {{C|Wraith Form}}, or {{C|Biased Cognition}}) is played to trigger {{R|Orange Pellets}}, then the [[Debuff]] will be removed (i.e. it will immediately remove the {{BD|Vulnerable}} for {{C|Berserk}}, remove the {{BD|Wraith Form}} for {{C|Wraith Form}}, and remove the {{BD|Bias}} for {{C|Biased Cognition}}).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Orange Pellets}} can be used to remove important [[Debuffs]] from some specific enemies, such as the {{BD|Vulnerable}} from {{M|Gremlin Nob}}'s {{Int|Gremlin Nob|Skull Bash}}, both the negative {{BD|Dexterity}} and negative {{BD|Strength}} from {{M|Lagavulin}}'s {{Int|Lagavulin|Siphon Soul}}, and all three of the {{BD|Vulnerable}}, {{BD|Weak}}, and {{BD|Frail}} from the {{M|Corrupt Heart}}'s {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Debilitate}}.\n* {{R|Orange Pellets}} can allow certain temporary [[Buffs]] to last the entire combat by removing the [[Debuff]] that would negate the buff at the end of the turn, such as the {{BD|Strength Down}} from {{P|Flex Potion}} and {{C|Flex}}, and the {{BD|Dexterity Down}} from {{P|Speed Potion}}.\n\n==== Positive Effects That Also Debuff the Player ====\n<!-- NOTE: If you add to (or remove from) this list, consider doing the same to Clockwork Souvenir for consistency purposes! -->\nThe following is a list of effects which generally offer some positive benefit, but also come with some sort of [[Debuff]] that can be removed by {{R|Orange Pellets}}. Note that the [[#Order|ordering]] of when {{R|Orange Pellets}} triggers means that, for any of the ''cards'' listed below, their [[Debuff]] will immediately be removed if they are the final card needed to trigger {{R|Orange Pellets}}.\n* {{C|Flex}} ({{BD|Strength Down}})\n* {{C|Battle Trance}} ({{BD|No Draw}})\n* {{C|Berserk}} ({{BD|Vulnerable}})\n* {{C|Bullet Time}} ({{BD|No Draw}})\n* {{C|Wraith Form}} ({{BD|Wraith Form}} and any negative {{BD|Dexterity}})\n* {{C|Reprogram}} (negative {{BD|Focus}})\n* {{C|Biased Cognition}} ({{BD|Bias}} and any negative {{BD|Focus}})\n* {{C|Hyperbeam}} (negative {{BD|Focus}})\n* {{C|Fasting}} ({{BD|Fasting}})\n* {{C|Panic Button}} ({{BD|No Block}})\n* {{R|Duality}} ({{BD|Dexterity Down}})\n* {{R|Mutagenic Strength}} ({{BD|Strength Down}})\n* {{P|Flex Potion}} ({{BD|Strength Down}})\n* {{P|Speed Potion}} ({{BD|Dexterity Down}})\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Orange Pellets}} does not remove the {{BD|Blasphemer}} effect gained from playing {{C|Blasphemy}} as it is specifically implemented as a [[Buff]] to make it more difficult to avoid.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* Unlike {{R|Clockwork Souvenir}}, {{R|Orange Pellets}} ''will'' remove the {{BD|Confused}} [[Debuff]] from {{R|Snecko Eye}}. This will essentially \"lock\" all of the randomized costs to whatever {{R|Snecko Eye}} set them to, as they will no longer be randomized when drawn again. While this can ''sometimes'' be beneficial if the player gets very lucky and many of their important cards are randomized to be very cheap, more often than not this will \"lock\" all of their unfavorable/expensive costs to stay expensive for the remainder of the combat. The player will usually have to be ''very'' careful about when and if they trigger {{R|Orange Pellets}} while they possess {{R|Snecko Eye}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The visual design of {{R|Orange Pellets}} is a reference to the [https://darksouls3.wiki.fextralife.com/Black+Bug+Pellet Black Bug Pellets], [https://darksouls3.wiki.fextralife.com/Red+Bug+Pellet Red Bug Pellets], and [https://darksouls3.wiki.fextralife.com/Yellow+Bug+Pellet Yellow Bug Pellets] from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Souls_III Dark Souls 3].\n** It may also be a reference to the similar [https://darksouls2.wiki.fextralife.com/Dark+Troches Dark Troches], [https://darksouls2.wiki.fextralife.com/Red+Leech+Troches Red Leech Troches], and [https://darksouls2.wiki.fextralife.com/Triclops+Snake+Troches Triclops Snake Troches] from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Souls_II Dark Souls 2].\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 35: Aggregate'''\n** Orange Pellets relic no longer has strange interactions with negative Focus.\n* '''Weekly Patch 34: Malaise'''\n** Oxford comma for Orange Pellets relic.\n* '''Weekly Patch 33: Terror'''\n** Adding relic Orange Pellets.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2396":{"pageid":2396,"ns":0,"title":"Ornamental Fan","revisions":[{"revid":55881,"parentid":50853,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Ornamental Fan\n|Image       = OrnamentalFan.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Every time you play 3 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} in a single turn, gain 4 {{KW|Block}}.\n|Flavor      = The fan seems to extend and harden as blood is spilled.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}}\n{{R|Ornamental Fan}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. {{R|Ornamental Fan}} can be triggered multiple times in the same turn, granting its {{KW|Block}} on the 3rd, 6th, 9th, etc. {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} played during the same turn.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Ornamental Fan}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Ornamental Fan}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Ornamental Fan}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Ornamental Fan}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Ornamental Fan}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Ornamental Fan}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Ornamental Fan}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Ornamental Fan}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Ornamental Fan}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Ornamental Fan}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Ornamental Fan}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\nBecause {{R|Ornamental Fan}} is not a [[Card]], it is unaffected by {{BD|Dexterity}}, {{BD|Frail}}, and {{BD|No Block}}. It will always grant 4 {{KW|Block}} when triggered.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Works especially well with decks that generate many low-cost or zero-cost {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}:\n** [[Ironclad]] can combine {{C|Anger}}, {{C|Twin Strike}}, and {{C|Whirlwind}} for multiple {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} per turn.\n** [[Silent]]'s shiv-generating cards like {{C|Blade Dance}}, {{C|Cloak and Dagger}}, and {{C|Infinite Blades}} allow frequent triggering of {{R|Ornamental Fan}}.\n** [[Defect]] can utilize {{C|Claw}} and other cheap {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} for repeated triggers.\n** [[Watcher]] can use {{C|Flurry of Blows}} and other stance-based {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} to reliably trigger it.\n* Works well with the other \"ninja relics\" which all interact with the number of {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} played in some way:\n** {{R|Shuriken}} grants {{BD|Strength}} under similar conditions.\n** {{R|Kunai}} grants {{BD|Dexterity}} under similar conditions, however {{R|Ornamental Fan}}'s {{KW|Block}} can't benefit from the {{BD|Dexterity}}.\n** {{R|Nunchaku}} provides extra energy every 10 {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}}, supporting attack-heavy decks.\n** {{R|Ninja Scroll}} allows for an easy trigger at the start of every combat.\n\n=== Anti-Synergy ===\n* If no other block-generating cards are played that turn, the effect of Ornamental Fan neutralises {{R|Orichalcum}}, resulting in 4 {{KW|Block}} rather than 6.\n* Decks that rely heavily on {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} or {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Powers}} (e.g. block-focused [[Defect]] with {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}, or [[Silent]] {{BD|Poison}} decks without {{C|Shiv|Shivs}}) may struggle to activate {{R|Ornamental Fan}} consistently.\n* When combined with effects that reduce card draw or limit playable cards (e.g. {{M|Time Eater}} or {{R|Velvet Choker}}), {{R|Ornamental Fan}}\u2019s usefulness is diminished.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Ornamental Fan}} is one of the relics in the \"ninja relics set\" which are all tools used in East-Asian Martial Arts and all synergize with a certain number of {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} played; the others being {{R|Shuriken}}, {{R|Kunai}}, {{R|Nunchaku}}, and {{R|Ninja Scroll}}.\n** Honorable mention to {{R|Pen Nib}} which fits the ''mechanical'' theme of the ninja relics, but has no actual ''visual'' theming with ninjas or East-Asian Martial Arts.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3085":{"pageid":3085,"ns":0,"title":"Pandora's Box","revisions":[{"revid":50925,"parentid":37223,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Pandora's Box\n|Image       = PandorasBox.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Upon pickup, {{KW|Transform}} all Strike and Defend cards.\n|Flavor      = You have a bad feeling about opening this.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Transform}}{{R|Pandora's Box}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Pandora's Box}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* {{R|Pandora's Box}} will never drop if any of these [[Custom Mode]] modifiers are active:\n** {{KW|Draft}}\n** {{KW|Sealed Deck}}\n** {{KW|Insanity}}\n** {{KW|Shiny}}\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Transform}} effects, {{R|Pandora's Box}} can be an effective form of pseudo-card-removal by having a reasonable likelihood of converting low-quality cards into higher quality cards.\n** For example, a random card offered by {{KW|Transform}} effects will often be better than keeping the player's remaining {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Strike|starter Strikes}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Defend|starter Defends}}.\n* The {{KW|Transform}} effect from {{R|Pandora's Box}} does not behave any differently from other {{KW|Transform}} effects. Therefore, any synergies with {{KW|Transform}} effects will also work with cards affected by {{R|Pandora's Box}}:\n** {{R|Ceramic Fish}} grants 9 [[Gold]] for each {{KW|Transform}}.\n** If a card {{KW|Transform|Transforms}} into a card type affected by one of {{R|Molten Egg}}, {{R|Toxic Egg}}, or {{R|Frozen Egg}}, the new card will be {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}}.\n\n== Bugs ==\nAn infamous bug with {{R|Pandora's Box}} remains in the game to this day despite multiple patch notes indicating it has been fixed at various points in the game's [[#Update History|Update History]]. The bug allows the player to remove all {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Strike|starter Strikes}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Defend|starter Defends}} ''without'' adding any of the {{KW|Transform|Transformed}} cards into their deck. This effectively acts as many free card removals without cluttering the player's deck with random cards.\n\nThe process of performing the bug is quite simple on the PC version<ref>It is possible to replicate the bug on the console and mobile ports of Slay the Spire, but this author does not know how. Please contribute this information if you know how to perform the bug on other platforms and input methods!</ref> of Slay the Spire:\n# Choose {{R|Pandora's Box}} as the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}} reward at the end of Act 1 or Act 2.\n## A screen will be presented to the player showing all of the cards that were {{KW|Transform|Transformed}}. There is a '''Confirm''' button in the bottom right, which the player should '''NOT''' click.\n## Notice that on the deck icon in the top right of the UI, the number of cards in the player's deck has been reduced by a significant amount. This is because all the {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Strike|starter Strikes}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Defend|starter Defends}} have already been removed from the deck, but the {{KW|Transform|Transformed}} cards have not been added yet.\n# Instead of clicking '''Confirm''' to add the {{KW|Transform|Transformed}} cards, open the '''Settings''' menu by clicking the Gear icon in the top right of the UI.\n# Click on the '''Abandon Run''' button.\n# Press the <code>Escape</code> keyboard button twice to close both the '''Abandon Run''' confirmation menu and the '''Settings''' menu.\n## The screen showing all of the {{KW|Transform|Transformed}} cards is now gone, and there is only the normal '''Proceed''' button remaining to continue to the next Act. The player's deck does not have any {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Strike|starter Strikes}}, {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:Defend|starter Defends}}, ''or'' the {{KW|Transform|Transformed}} cards in it.\n# Click '''Proceed''' to continue into the next Act without picking up any of the {{KW|Transform|Transformed}} cards.\n\nThis bug has been used to great success in many speedruns of Slay the Spire to quickly reduce the player's deck to only a few key cards that can be played to win fights almost instantly. [https://youtu.be/s7mPIsyK4BA?t=90 Here is an example of the bug being performed] in the (at time of writing) world record human-performed speedrun of Slay the Spire by Zee/Zelda19. Although the glitch is performed extremely quickly using a controller so that the process cannot be seen properly, the effect is the same as outlined above.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Pandora's Box is an artifact in Greek mythology from the myth of Pandora. In the myth, Pandora is given a storage jar that she opens to unleash a number of curses upon mankind, such as sickness, death, and many other unspecified evils.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pandora%27s_box</ref>\n** The flavor text of {{R|Pandora's Box}} references this myth; ''\"You have a bad feeling about opening this.\"''\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 53: Blizzard'''\n** Crash fix when looking at Deck, Map, or Settings after getting Pandora's Box at Neow.\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Updated relic asset for Pandora's Box.\n** Fixed issue where Pandora's Box relic couldn't transform cards to Rare.\n* '''Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act'''\n** Daily mods that alter the starting deck like Sealed Deck and Draft now eliminate Pandora's Box as it does nothing.\n* '''Weekly Pact 48: Creative AI'''\n** Added logic to remove the Strike tip from Pandora's box (attempt 2).\n* '''Weekly Patch 33: Terror'''\n** Obtaining Pandora's Box without Strikes or Defends no longer opens a blank screen.\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** Game now saves after receiving cards from Pandora's Box and Astrolabe via Neow so your cards don't vanish.\n* '''Weekly Patch 21: Channel Lightning'''\n** Adding apostrophe to Pandora's Box.\n* '''Weekly Patch 20: Immolate'''\n** Potential fix for Pandora's Box and Calling Bell issues and exploits.\n** Typo fix in Pandora's Box helper text.\n* '''Weekly Patch 19: Seed'''\n** Potential fix for Calling Bell and Pandora's Box edge case issues.\n* '''Weekly Patch 18: Paranormal'''\n** Fix for Calling Bell/Library/Pandora's Box exploit.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2528":{"pageid":2528,"ns":0,"title":"Pen Nib","revisions":[{"revid":55893,"parentid":50819,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Pen Nib\n|Image       = PenNib.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Every 10th {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} you play deals double damage.\n|Flavor      = Holding the nib, you can see everyone ever slain by a previous owner of the pen. A violent history.\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Pen Nib}}{{R|Pen Nib}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. The counter that keeps track of how many {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} have been played is not reset between turns or combats.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Pen Nib}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Pen Nib}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Pen Nib}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Pen Nib}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Pen Nib}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Pen Nib}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Pen Nib}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Pen Nib}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Pen Nib}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Pen Nib}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\nWhen calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Pen Nib}}'s {{BD|Pen Nib}} buff is a multiplicative bonus, and stacks with {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Ninja Scroll}} allows for an easy +3 to {{R|Pen Nib}}'s counter at the start of every combat.\n* {{R|Pen Nib}}'s buff applies to all hits from a single card, allowing multi-hit {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attacks}} (such as {{C|Sword Boomerang}}, {{C|Flechettes}}, and {{C|Bowling Bash}}) to benefit from the increased damage for all their hits.\n* Cards which generate multiple cheap {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} (such as {{C|Blade Dance}} and {{C|Storm of Steel}}) can contribute a lot of progress to {{R|Pen Nib}}'s counter.\n** These cheap {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} can be used to build up {{R|Pen Nib}}'s buff and then use it on a single devastating '''attack''', such as {{C|Ragnarok}} or {{C|Immolate}}.\n* {{P|Attack Potion}} can be used flexibly to either find a random {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} that will activate {{R|Pen Nib}}'s buff for the next '''attack''', or find a big '''attack''' to take advantage of the buff itself.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{C|Havoc}}, {{C|Mayhem}}, and {{P|Distilled Chaos}} can be used to potentially play a less desirable {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} from the draw pile and activate {{R|Pen Nib}}'s buff to be used on a more effective '''attack''' currently in the hand.\n** This can potentially backfire if the player is attempting to dig for a big attack from their draw pile while the buff is active and ends up playing a low-damage attack instead, somewhat wasting the damage bonus.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Pen Nib}} resembles the nib of a dip pen.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dip_pen</ref> It is a type of pen that must be physically dipped into ink, and the nib is responsible for depositing a small amount of ink onto the writing surface.\n** Dip pens have their origins as far back as ancient Egypt, and are still manufactured and used today.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 47: Metamorphosis'''\n** Relics with counters like Pen Nib and Girya no longer display the counter offset incorrectly when the player has too many relics.\n* '''Weekly Patch 42: Reboot'''\n** Updating icon for Pen Nib power.\n* '''Weekly Patch 5: Happy Holidays!'''\n** Pen Nib and Double Damage (Phantasmal Killer) now stack as they're separate powers.\n** Pen Nib power gets a new icon (temporary).\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3084":{"pageid":3084,"ns":0,"title":"Philosopher's Stone","revisions":[{"revid":55895,"parentid":50927,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Philosopher's Stone\n|Image       = PhilosophersStone.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. ALL enemies start combat with 1 {{BD|Strength}}.\n|Flavor      = Raw energy emanates from the stone, empowering all nearby.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{Power Infobox|Strength}}{{R|Philosopher's Stone}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* The {{BD|Strength}} buff from {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} is applied to enemies immediately when combat begins. They appear to have the {{BD|Strength}} already applied to them by the time their HP and buffs are visible.\n* If new enemies enter combat mid-way through (such as from {{M|Gremlin Leader}} or {{M|Reptomancer}} summoning [[Minions]]), then the new enemies will also receive the {{BD|Strength}} buff immediately after they're summoned. They appear to enter combat with the {{BD|Strength}} already applied to them.\n** This is in contrast to {{R|Preserved Insect}} which only affects enemies that are present during the start of combat, and has no effect on enemies summoned mid-way through.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* The drawback of {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} can be lessened with damage mitigation tools that are effective against enemies that can make good use of the additional {{BD|Strength}}, such as {{C|Disarm}}, {{C|Piercing Wail}}, {{C|Go for the Eyes}}, and {{C|Wave of the Hand}}.\n* {{R|Tungsten Rod}} can somewhat counteract the + {{BD|Strength}} gain from {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} by reducing all HP loss by 1. Note that this does not counteract the effect of additional {{BD|Strength}} on {{KW|Block|Blocked}} damage.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* The additional {{BD|Strength}} granted to enemies from {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} is at its most dangerous in multi-enemy fights, or fights where enemies frequently multi-attack.\n** The {{M|Byrd|Byrds}} in particular are notorious for becoming significantly more deadly when the player has {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} as they can show up very early in Act 2.\n** Other examples of dangerous enemies with {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} include {{M|Snake Plant}}, {{M|Book of Stabbing}}, {{M|Gremlin Leader}} and their [[Gremlins|Gremlin Minions]], {{M|Reptomancer}}, and the {{M|Corrupt Heart}} (especially from its {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Blood Shots}} attack).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The philosopher's stone is a mythic alchemical substance capable of turning basic metals, such as lead or mercury, into precious metals, such as gold or silver. It was also believed to be a key component in creating an \"elixir of life\" which was said to grant immortality.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosopher%27s_stone</ref> It is not always referenced as a literal stone or crystal, and has also been described as being a liquid or fine powder throughout history.\n* While the philosopher's stone has existed in real-world myth for roughly 1700 years, the visual design of {{R|Philosopher's Stone}} somewhat resembles one of the many designs of the [https://fma.fandom.com/wiki/Philosopher%27s_Stone same item from the popular Full Metal Alchemist series], which was partly responsible for popularizing the myth of the philosopher's stone in the modern day.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 54: Loop'''\n** Fixed issues where some monsters dealt the incorrect damage with Philosopher's Stone on turn 1.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Reptomancer's Daggers and Philosopher's Stone relic now correctly interact.\n* '''Weekly Patch 49: The Designer'''\n** Philosopher's Stone buffed: Enemies gain 2 Strength -> 1 Strength.\n** Philosopher's Stone wording improvements.\n* '''Weekly Patch 40: Forethought'''\n** Spawned Daggers from Reptomancer will now correctly work with Philosopher's Stone, and mods like Lethality.\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** Adding Relic: Philosopher's Stone (was locked via char 3)\n** Philosopher's Stone relic wording clarified\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2531":{"pageid":2531,"ns":0,"title":"Preserved Insect","revisions":[{"revid":56875,"parentid":56096,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Preserved Insect\n|Image       = PreservedInsect.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Enemies in Elite combats have 25% less HP.\n|Flavor      = The insect seems to create a shrinking aura that targets particularly large enemies.\n}}\n{{R|Preserved Insect}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. It reduces the HP of enemies in [[Elite]] encounters by 25%. The Max HP of the elite enemies are unaffected by the HP penalty; instead their current HP immediately lowers at the start of combat as if they had taken damage.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Preserved Insect}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Preserved Insect}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Preserved Insect}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Preserved Insect}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Preserved Insect}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Preserved Insect}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Preserved Insect}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Preserved Insect}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Preserved Insect}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Preserved Insect}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 53 (the [[Acts#Act 4: The Ending|Act 4]] [[Shop]], or the {{Asc|20|Act 4 Rest Site due to the extra floor from A20's double boss}}).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\nIf an [[Elite]] combat has {{BD|Minion|Minions}} at the start of it (namely the [[Gremlins]] in the {{M|Gremlin Leader}} encounter, and the {{M|Dagger|Daggers}} in the {{M|Reptomancer}} encounter), the {{BD|Minion|Minions}} will be affected by {{R|Preserved Insect}}'s HP penalty. However, any {{BD|Minion|Minions}} that are summoned ''after'' the start of combat will be unaffected by the penalty and enter with full HP.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Sling of Courage}} grants the player +2 {{BD|Strength}} in [[Elite]] combats, and can allow {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}}-heavy decks to kill [[Elites]] more easily.\n* {{R|Black Star}} drops an extra [[Relic]] after winning an [[Elite]] combat, making these fights more rewarding.\n* {{R|Slaver's Collar}} grants the player 1 additional {{KW|Energy}} in [[Elite]] combats, and can allow the player to kill [[Elites]] more easily.\n\n== Bugs ==\n* {{R|Preserved Insect}} has no bugs as the insect is a beetle (order ''Coleoptera''), which are not true bugs (order ''Hemiptera'').<ref>https://sciencenotes.org/difference-between-bugs-and-insects/</ref>\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The non-{{BD|Minion}} enemies in [[Elite]] combats will be visually smaller when affected by {{R|Preserved Insect}}'s HP penalty.\n* The depicted insect reappears in ''Slay the Spire 2'' as the {{M2|Shrinker Beetle}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Preserved Insect relic no longer spawns beyond floor 52 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 47: Metamorphosis'''\n** Updated description for Preserved Insect for clarity.\n* '''Weekly Patch 46: Adrenaline'''\n** Preserved Insect and Sling should now work in all elite combats including elite battles from events (like dead adventurer event).\n** When using Preserved Insect, affected enemies are rendered 30% smaller.\n* '''Weekly Patch 42: Reboot'''\n** Fixed issue where Lagavulin would come out of its shell with Neow's Lament or Preserved Insect.\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n** New Common Relic: Preserved Insect.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2122":{"pageid":2122,"ns":0,"title":"Runic Capacitor","revisions":[{"revid":55786,"parentid":55431,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Runic Capacitor\n|Image       = RunicCapacitor.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Character   = Defect\n|Description = Start each combat with 3 additional {{KW|Orb}} slots.\n|Flavor      = More is better.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Orb Slot}}{{Keyword Infobox|Orb}}{{R|Runic Capacitor}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Defect|Defect-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]]. The [[Defect]] will start combat with 6 {{KW|Orb Slot|Orb Slots}} instead of the normal 3. The maximum number of {{KW|Orb Slot|Orb Slots}} is 10.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Defect]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{C|Barrage}} can deal a higher maximum damage with more {{KW|Orb Slot|Orb Slots}}.\n* {{C|Fission}} can be used for more draw, {{Icon|BE}} Energy, and {{KW|Evoke|Evoked}} {{KW|Orb|Orbs}}.\n* {{C|Compile Driver}} draws an additional card for each different type of {{KW|Orb}} {{KW|Channel|Channeled}}; more {{KW|Orb Slot|Orb Slots}} allows the maximum draw of 4 cards, as well as making it easier to keep multiple {{KW|Orb}} types {{KW|Channel|Channeled}}.\n* More {{KW|Orb Slot|Orb Slots}} may benefit all {{KW|Orb}} generation sources, but especially ones which can fill slots quickly, such as {{C|Tempest}}, {{C|Chill}}, {{C|Glacier}}, {{C|Rainbow}}, and {{C|Electrodynamics}}.\n* {{C|Consume}} can be used more times with additional {{KW|Orb Slot|Orb Slots}}, allowing for large {{BD|Focus}} gain.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* If the player does not have many ways of {{KW|Evoke|Evoking}} their {{KW|Orb|Orbs}} on-demand from cards such as {{C|Recursion}} or {{C|Multi-Cast}}, they may find it more difficult than usual to forcefully {{KW|Evoke}} their front {{KW|Orb}} by {{KW|Channel|Channeling}} enough {{KW|Orb|Orbs}} to exceed their limit.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A capacitor is a device that stores electricity by accumulating electrical charges from two electrically-charged surfaces that are close together but insulated from each other.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capacitor</ref> They are a commonly used component in many electrical circuits and circuit boards as a form of energy storage.\n* {{R|Runic Capacitor}} is part of the \"runic relic set\" alongside {{R|Runic Cube}}, {{R|Runic Dome}}, and {{R|Runic Pyramid}}. Unlike most other \"relic sets\", the runic relics have no common mechanic and are purely related by name and visual design.\n** {{R|Runic Capacitor}} is the only {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Defect|Defect-exclusive}} runic relic.\n** {{R|Runic Capacitor}} is the only runic relic that is not {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss rarity}}.\n** {{R|Runic Capacitor}} is the only runic relic where its runes are not present on {{R|Stone Calendar}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 27: Hello World'''\n** This week adds the long anticipated third character into the game!\n** Content: 9 Relics.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1532":{"pageid":1532,"ns":0,"title":"Shovel","revisions":[{"revid":50891,"parentid":37252,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Shovel\n|Image       = Shovel.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = You can now Dig for relics at Rest Sites.\n|Flavor      = The Spire houses all number of relics from past civilizations and powerful adventurers lost to time. Time to go dig them up!\n}}\n{{R|Shovel}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. It adds a \"Dig\" option to [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]], which allows the player to receive a random [[Relic]] that is {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, or {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}}. The random relic can't be a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}}, {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}}, or {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} relic.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Shovel}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Shovel}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Shovel}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Shovel}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Shovel}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Shovel}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Shovel}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Shovel}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Shovel}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Shovel}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Shovel}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Shovel}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n* Only '''two''' relics that add an option to [[Rest Site|Rest Sites]] ({{R|Girya}}, {{R|Peace Pipe}}, and {{R|Shovel}}) can be offered to the player during a run. Once two have been offered, the third will no longer be accessible by any means.\n* {{R|Black Star}}'s extra relic can never drop a relic that adds an option to [[Rest Site|Rest Sites]] (like {{R|Shovel}}).\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Shovel}}'s '''Dig''' option can be provide the player with something to do at [[Rest Site|Rest Sites]] if they do not have any particularly good {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} to '''Smith''' and they're comfortable with their amount of HP and don't need to '''Rest'''.\n** Also applicable if the player has {{R|Fusion Hammer}} and/or {{R|Coffee Dripper}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* In runs where the player has seen every [[Relic]] (such as in an [[Endless|Endless run]]), digging with {{R|Shovel}} will always yield a {{R|Circlet}}.\n* {{R|Shovel}}'s visual design is a reference to the shovel weapon wielded by [https://www.yachtclubgames.com/games/shovel-knight-shovel-of-hope Shovel Knight].\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Fixed issue that caused Shovel, Girya, and Peace Pipe to incorrectly spawn when they should not.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Shovel relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 16: Back to Work'''\n** Shovel relic now uses reward screen so that you can skip the relic if you do not want it.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3081":{"pageid":3081,"ns":0,"title":"Sozu","revisions":[{"revid":50935,"parentid":37260,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Sozu\n|Image       = Sozu.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. You can no longer obtain [[Potions|potions]].\n|Flavor      = You notice that magical liquids seem to lose their properties when near this relic.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Sozu}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. It prevents [[Potions]] from being acquired, but any [[Potions]] the player has before acquiring {{R|Sozu}} can still be used as normal.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Sozu}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Sozu}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* While the drawback of {{R|Sozu}} is typically quite severe, the player may find that being unable to pick up [[Potions]] is manageable if their deck is well-rounded and able to handle a wide variety of upcoming enemies without needing to use [[Potions]] as a \"safety net\".\n** For example, decks that are capable of reliably setting up infinite combos (such as the [[Sundial#Bugs|Sundial infinite combo]]), or that can reliably find the cards needed for specific turns (making effective use of cards such as {{C|Dark Embrace}}, {{C|Acrobatics}}, {{C|Seek}}, and {{C|Rushdown}}) may find that the drawback of {{R|Sozu}} is manageable and the increased {{KW|Energy}} gain allows them to execute their strategies without needing to rely on alternative {{KW|Energy}} generation methods.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} will still work as expected for the player's remaining short supply of [[Potions]].\n* The [[Merchant]] will still stock [[Potions]], but attempting to buy them will do nothing and the player will not lose any [[Gold]]. {{R|Sozu}} will \"flash\" in the player's relic bar whenever they attempt to buy a [[Potion]] in this way.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{C|Alchemize}} will no longer grant a [[Potion]] on play.\n* The guaranteed [[Potion]] drop from {{R|White Beast Statue}} cannot be picked up.\n* {{R|Cauldron}} can still be purchased from the [[Merchant]], but none of the potions can be picked up. It is effectively a waste of [[Gold]].\n* {{P|Entropic Brew}} will not grant any [[Potions]] when consumed.\n* {{E|Knowing Skull}}'s {{Frame|A Pick Me Up?}} will still cause the player to lose HP, but they will not gain a [[Potion]].\n* In general, many decks can rely on [[Potions]] to help manage their weaknesses against specific fights which target that weakness (such as {{P|Explosive Potion}} helping take out multiple [[Gremlins]] in the fight against {{M|Gremlin Leader}} when the player lacks AoE damage). Being unable to pickup [[Potions]] will often require the player's deck to be able to handle a much wider variety of scenarios without being able to rely on the \"safety net\" that [[Potions]] often provide.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A sozu is a device used traditionally in Japan to scare away animals that pose a threat to agriculture, such as deer and boars. It achieves this by resting a short length of bamboo on a pivot point which is repeatedly filled with water and emptied to quickly strike the bamboo against a rock. This creates a loud noise which disrupts the typical silence of a garden and frightens approaching animals.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shishi-odoshi</ref>\n** The general term for this class of \"animal-prevention device\" is ''shishi-odoshi'', which can also include devices such as scarecrows.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Knowing Skull event can no longer give you a potion if you have the Sozu relic.\n* '''Weekly Patch 13: Thinking Ahead'''\n** Sozu now prevents you from acquiring new potions rather than preventing usage (similar to Ectoplasm).\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3018":{"pageid":3018,"ns":0,"title":"Teardrop Locket","revisions":[{"revid":56620,"parentid":50870,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Teardrop Locket\n| Image       = TeardropLocket.png\n| Rarity      = Uncommon\n| Character   = Watcher\n| Description = Start each combat in {{KW|Calm}}.\n| Flavor      = Its owner blind, its contents unseen.\n| GivesToPlayer = Calm\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Calm}}\n{{R|Teardrop Locket}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Watcher|Watcher-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Teardrop Locket}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Teardrop Locket}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Teardrop Locket}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Teardrop Locket}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Teardrop Locket}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Teardrop Locket}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Teardrop Locket}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Teardrop Locket}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Teardrop Locket}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Teardrop Locket}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Teardrop Locket}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained by [[Watcher]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Any {{KW|Stance}}-swapping effect (such as {{C|Eruption}}, {{C|Empty Mind}}, or {{C|Blasphemy}}) can provide the {{KW|Energy}} from exiting {{KW|Calm}} more easily since the player won't need to find a {{KW|Calm}}-entering card beforehand.\n* {{C|Like Water}} can be activated on turn 1 in the case that a {{KW|Calm}}-entering card was not drawn.\n* {{C|Inner Peace}} can be used to draw cards on turn 1 more easily, allowing the player to dig further into their deck earlier into the fight.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Watcher only, Uncommon Relic: Teardrop Locket\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3020":{"pageid":3020,"ns":0,"title":"White Beast Statue","revisions":[{"revid":50862,"parentid":37293,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = White Beast Statue\n|Image       = WhiteBeastStatue.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = [[Potions]] always appear in combat rewards.\n|Flavor      = A small white statue of a creature you have never seen before.\n}}\n{{R|White Beast Statue}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]]. The extra [[Potions|potion]] will be awarded during the combat rewards screen as normal.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|White Beast Statue}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|White Beast Statue}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|White Beast Statue}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|White Beast Statue}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|White Beast Statue}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|White Beast Statue}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|White Beast Statue}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|White Beast Statue}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|White Beast Statue}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|White Beast Statue}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|White Beast Statue}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* May allow {{R|Toy Ornithopter}} to provide its healing more often, as [[Potions]] can generally be used more liberally when {{R|White Beast Statue}} guarantees as least 1 in every combat reward.\n* All of the extra [[Potions]] will have their effects improved by {{R|Sacred Bark}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Sozu}} will render {{R|White Beast Statue}} useless.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** White Beast Statue relic rarity changed from Boss -> Uncommon.\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** Fixed issue where White Beast relic would still spawn on Brewmaster Daily.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]}}}}